id prince recognised by the Abbasid caliph as prince of princes of Armenia in 862.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashot-i-of-iberia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ashot-i-of-iberia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ashot I of Iberia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ashot I of Iberia was the Bagratid prince who established his family as the leading power in Georgia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashot-ii-of-armenia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ashot-ii-of-armenia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ashot II of Armenia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ashot II, called Yerkat, 'the Iron', was king of Bagratid Armenia from 914 to 929.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashot-iii-of-armenia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ashot-iii-of-armenia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ashot III of Armenia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ashot III, called the Merciful, was king of Bagratid Armenia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashot-msaker"><span class="enc-name">Ashot Msaker</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ashot Msaker, the Carnivore, was a Bagratid prince who became presiding prince of Armenia under the Abbasid Caliphate in the early 9th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashraf-ghani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ashraf-ghani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ashraf Ghani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ashraf Ghani is an Afghan academic and politician who served as president of the Islamic Republic of Afghanistan from 2014 to 2021, at first sharing power with Abdullah…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashraf-hotak"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ashraf-hotak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ashraf Hotak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ashraf Hotak was an Afghan ruler of the Hotak dynasty who reigned as shah in Persia from 1725, after deposing his cousin Mahmud Hotak.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-ashraf-khalil"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-ashraf-khalil.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al-Ashraf Khalil</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Ashraf Khalil was the Mamluk sultan of Egypt and Syria from 1290, son of Qalawun.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-ashraf-musa-sultan-of-damascus"><span class="enc-name">Al-Ashraf Musa (Ayyubid sultan of Damascus)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Ashraf Musa was an Ayyubid prince, son of al-Adil, who ruled the Jazira from Harran and later became Sultan of Damascus in 1229.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-ashraf-musa-emir-of-homs"><span class="enc-name">Al-Ashraf Musa, Emir of Homs</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Ashraf Musa was the last Ayyubid emir of Homs.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-ashraf-umar-ii"><span class="enc-name">Al-Ashraf Umar II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Ashraf Umar II was a sultan of the Rasulid dynasty of Yemen who reigned briefly from 1295 to 1296, after his father al-Muzaffar Yusuf I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashta-pradhan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ashta-pradhan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ashta Pradhan</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ashta Pradhan was the council of eight ministers established by Shivaji, founder of the Maratha Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashtur"><span class="enc-name">Ashtur</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ashtur is a village near Bidar in Karnataka, India, site of the tombs of the Bahmani sultans who ruled from Bidar after the 1420s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashur-god"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ashur-god.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ashur (god)</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Ashur was the chief god of the Assyrians, originally the deity of the city of Ashur on the Tigris, with which he shared his name.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashur-nadin-shumi"><span class="enc-name">Ashur-nadin-shumi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ashur-nadin-shumi was the eldest son of the Assyrian king Sennacherib, who installed him as king of Babylon in 700 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashur-nirari-v"><span class="enc-name">Ashur-nirari V</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ashur-nirari V was king of Assyria from 755 to 745 BCE, at a low point of Assyrian power, when provincial governors were largely independent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashur-uballit-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ashur-uballit-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ashur-uballit I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ashur-uballit I was king of Assyria from 1365 to 1330 BCE.</span></a></